1
00:00:00,116 --> 00:00:02,763
Hello and welcome back to J D Science.

2
00:00:02,914 --> 00:00:05,573
Today, we are exploring cell division.

3
00:00:05,909 --> 00:00:09,520
Every nucleus contains chromosomes made of D N

4
00:00:09,671 --> 00:00:13,061
A, and each chromosome carries many genes.

5
00:00:13,270 --> 00:00:17,229
In mitosis, the cell first copies its D N A and

6
00:00:17,276 --> 00:00:18,913
then divides it into two.

7
00:00:19,191 --> 00:00:22,825
Each daughter cell has the same chromosomes as the parent,

8
00:00:22,907 --> 00:00:25,310
so mitosis produces clones.

9
00:00:25,519 --> 00:00:29,907
It is used for growth, repair, and asexual reproduction.

10
00:00:30,186 --> 00:00:32,438
Stem cells have not yet specialised.

11
00:00:32,613 --> 00:00:36,304
They are found in embryos and bone marrow, and in plants

12
00:00:36,479 --> 00:00:38,325
they are found in meristems.

13
00:00:38,777 --> 00:00:42,620
Therapeutic cloning could produce an embryo with the patient's

14
00:00:42,702 --> 00:00:46,614
own genes, which may reduce the risk of rejection.

15
00:00:47,229 --> 00:00:50,492
The use of stem cells raises ethical debates.

16
00:00:50,887 --> 00:00:54,950
Before a cell divides, its D N A is replicated

17
00:00:55,124 --> 00:00:56,355
during interphase.

18
00:00:56,773 --> 00:01:00,674
During mitosis, the duplicated chromosomes are separated

19
00:01:00,906 --> 00:01:04,900
so that each new nucleus receives an equivalent set.

20
00:01:05,213 --> 00:01:08,487
The cytoplasm then divides in cytokinesis.

21
00:01:08,905 --> 00:01:12,678
Remember that mitosis maintains chromosome number; it does

22
00:01:12,736 --> 00:01:13,503
not halve it.

23
00:01:13,897 --> 00:01:18,274
In humans, most body cells have forty-six chromosomes,

24
00:01:18,483 --> 00:01:22,477
and each daughter cell normally has forty-six too.

25
00:01:22,930 --> 00:01:26,692
As you revise this topic, try to explain the key process

26
00:01:26,761 --> 00:01:27,923
in your own words.

27
00:01:28,318 --> 00:01:32,311
Focus on what happens, why it happens, and how the structures

28
00:01:32,381 --> 00:01:34,192
involved help the process work.

29
00:01:34,645 --> 00:01:38,198
Now, pause the video and try this exam-style question.

30
00:01:38,650 --> 00:01:41,797
Describe what happens to the D N A in a cell before

31
00:01:41,832 --> 00:01:43,051
mitosis begins.

32
00:01:43,759 --> 00:01:46,208
[two marks] Here is a model answer.

33
00:01:46,359 --> 00:01:50,481
The D N A is copied (replicated), so each chromosome

34
00:01:50,539 --> 00:01:53,952
has two identical copies before the cell divides.

